00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:31.664
So to kick us off, how do
you define data science?

00:06:31.664 --> 00:06:32.580
JULIET HOUGLAND: Sure.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:35.910
So Stitch Fix has
a department called

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:37.920
algorithms and analytics.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.620
And the algorithms and
analytics department

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:43.140
is vertically oriented.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.680
Meaning we work with styling.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:46.761
We work with clients.

00:06:46.761 --> 00:06:48.510
We work with merchandising
and operations,

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:50.100
like our warehouse operations.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:54.750
And the data scientists on our
team do a variety of things

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:57.150
but try to enable and
optimize the business

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.740
through using data well.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.530
And we err on the side of trying
to do that problematically

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:05.610
and automatically as opposed
to having one of our business

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:07.140
partners come to
a data scientist

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:08.880
and say, hey, like,
I have this question.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.260
Build me a report.

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:11.910
So a lot of what our
data scientists do

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:15.690
is try to build systems and
levers into the business that

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.490
help the business run more
efficiently and optimize it.

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:22.530
So a great example is warehouse
routing and optimization

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:24.870
of how do people that
are picking items

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:27.122
of like [INAUDIBLE]
clothing and off of racks

00:07:27.122 --> 00:07:29.580
walk through the warehouse to
minimize the distance, right?

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:32.642
So that's not a definition,
but that's how we practice it.

00:07:32.642 --> 00:07:33.600
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:34.974
And I've seen
different companies

00:07:34.974 --> 00:07:38.110
have different definitions
for what that means for them.

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:40.331
So Michelle, how do you
define data science?

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:40.950
MICHELE CASBON: Yeah.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:42.991
Actually this is one of
the most loaded questions

00:07:42.991 --> 00:07:44.490
that I ever get.

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:48.900
Like Juliet, so my back on has
also been heavy engineering.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.700
Started out with
computer science degrees

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:52.980
and kind of gone back and
forth between data science,

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:56.220
like official data science roles
and official engineering rules.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:59.860
And there's been a lot of
variability in those roles

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:02.120
but the same person and
mostly the same skill

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:03.870
set for all the
different titles and all

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.160
the different responsibilities.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:07.936
So I feel like,
exactly as you said,

00:08:07.936 --> 00:08:09.810
every company has their
own definition of it.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:11.700
And it's kind of
grown organically.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:13.777
It's still like a
fairly young field

00:08:13.777 --> 00:08:16.110
and going through especially
data science interviews are

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:19.350
one of the most confusing
things to prepare for because

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:22.950
of those differing definitions
and the different expectations.

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:24.870
I feel like it's
definitely blending more

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:27.720
into there's less of a
differentiation between data

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:29.344
engineering and data science.

00:08:29.344 --> 00:08:31.260
Mostly I feel like it's
just an umbrella term.

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.010
And it's a way for
people to describe hey,

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:35.051
there's this magical thing
that I want to happen,

00:08:35.051 --> 00:08:36.720
and I need people
with math skills,

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:38.429
with engineering
skills, people who

00:08:38.429 --> 00:08:41.414
can actually build something
to solve a particular problem.

00:08:41.414 --> 00:08:43.289
But there's a lot of
contention in this area,

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:45.900
and specifically I
feel like it kind

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:49.081
of fuels the fire of data
science versus engineering

00:08:49.081 --> 00:08:50.830
that I think is kind
of counterproductive.

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:54.524
So I'm going to decline from
giving a specific definition.

00:08:54.524 --> 00:08:55.440
JULIET HOUGLAND: Yeah.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:58.410
And I think even within
what you're describing,

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:00.380
I've heard data
science decomposed

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:02.070
into three areas
of data science,

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:05.550
where you have
inference and A/B test,

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:08.100
you have decision
science, like how

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:11.700
do you come to some sort
of business conclusion

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:13.830
or decide what a
business should do,

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:16.560
and then you also have the
sort of machine learning

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:19.800
engineering
building-automatic-systems type

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:20.969
of data science.

00:09:20.969 --> 00:09:23.010
And so I think those are
three categories of what

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:25.500
data scientists do that
require different skill sets

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:26.220
and experience.

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:29.040
And you can get
deep in any of them.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:30.960
And what you're describing
with engineering,

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:33.250
I think all of them
benefit from engineering.

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:36.224
But some have better tooling
already and some don't.

00:09:36.224 --> 00:09:37.890
Decision science and
inference, I think,

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:41.467
have relatively good
tooling in existence.

00:09:41.467 --> 00:09:43.050
Whereas the machinery
and engineering,

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.310
the tooling is kind
of lacking and new.

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:45.960
MICHELE CASBON:
Yeah, that's true.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:47.543
And it requires a
lot more engineering

00:09:47.543 --> 00:09:49.310
in order to enable that area.

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:50.160
JULIET HOUGLAND:
Yeah, definitely.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:51.420
MARK MANDEL: You
mentioned there's tooling.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.610
What sort of tooling does
exist for those areas

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:55.200
that you're
particularly fond of?

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:57.616
MICHELE CASBON: I mean it's
getting better over the years,

00:09:57.616 --> 00:09:59.700
but it's been pretty lacking.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:02.380
I guess traditionally the
decision science tools,

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:06.000
things like SaaS, Matlab, things
that are more heavy on the math

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:09.450
side, but actually putting
those into production systems,

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:12.450
we've kind of seen a shift
from traditional software

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:15.640
engineering tools that have been
sort of adapted over the years

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:18.740
to things that are more
specific to do science.

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:23.030
I guess in the past I've
seen things like Informatica,

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:27.260
just data fork lifting tools,
back-end systems like Teradata

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:32.760
to try and solve the distributed
compute problem, up to today.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:35.060
The biggest shift I
have seen in the space

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:37.190
is with Kubernetes
and being able to take

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:39.380
a lot of those very
specific problems

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:41.870
and run them in a
single environment.

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:43.340
That's where I've
seen a big shift

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:46.700
from these sort of very
specific science tools

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:50.369
solving one specific problem
into a more general purpose

00:10:50.369 --> 00:10:52.160
fitting more into that
traditional software

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:55.670
development role, where you
have all these pieces that

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:59.120
can interact together
in a repeatable way.

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:01.716
And you can deploy it,
and you can be confident

00:11:01.716 --> 00:11:03.590
that that code hasn't
changed and that things

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:04.820
haven't changed in between.

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:06.080
And you can be a lot
more robust and sort of

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:08.330
adopt those traditional
software development practices

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:10.581
into more of the data science,
machine learning space.

00:11:10.581 --> 00:11:11.496
JULIET HOUGLAND: Yeah.

00:11:11.496 --> 00:11:13.670
I've seen-- I think there's
a ton of different tools

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:17.340
out there at different levels
of the data stack, right?

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:20.550
There's a ton of new
data processing libraries

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:21.800
that run on people's machines.

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:24.520
So scikit-learn has been
around for a while, but a ton--

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:27.260
like xgboost is getting a
huge amount of traction,

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:29.150
things like PyTorch
and TensorFlow,

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:30.230
huge amount of traction.

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:32.180
Those are booming.

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:34.250
And they often require
specialized hardware.

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:35.708
But that brings up
the question of,

00:11:35.708 --> 00:11:37.719
once you have these
programs running

00:11:37.719 --> 00:11:39.760
and a data scientist has
built some sort of model

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:41.560
that works on their laptop,
that's not production.

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:44.101
We need to be able to actually
use the infrastructure that we

00:11:44.101 --> 00:11:46.490
have and put these things
on some sort of schedule

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:48.440
and know where these are
going to get executed

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:50.070
and in what environment.

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.220
And so there's a huge
amount of tooling around

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:54.553
how do you take something
that works on someone's laptop

00:11:54.553 --> 00:11:56.900
and make it robust,
reliable, scheduled?

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:58.670
And I think things
like Kubernetes--

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:00.290
or at Stitch Fix
we have a system

00:12:00.290 --> 00:12:04.260
called Flotilla, which is
used for defining a job

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:05.880
and what that job
might look like.

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:07.456
And so a job has a Docker image.

00:12:07.456 --> 00:12:08.330
It has a run command.

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:10.288
It has environment
variables, things like that.

00:12:10.288 --> 00:12:13.045
And beyond this job definition,
you also want scheduling.

00:12:13.045 --> 00:12:14.420
And once you have
scheduling, you

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:16.980
have to begin to think about,
well, this is not one person

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:18.230
scheduling something.

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:19.760
This is actually
a variety of teams

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:20.990
that have a variety of needs.

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:22.730
And they're all
sharing a resource.

00:12:22.730 --> 00:12:24.188
And you begin to
pretty quickly get

00:12:24.188 --> 00:12:26.570
into queues and
scheduling and what

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:29.540
it means for having
some sort of fair share

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:32.510
between different teams on
cluster resources that we have.

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:36.002
And so the things that have
been solved on supercomputers,

00:12:36.002 --> 00:12:37.460
then solving for
resource managers,

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:39.777
you end up beginning to
think about your own systems.

00:12:39.777 --> 00:12:42.110
So I think there's lots of
different places in the stack

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:43.770
where new tools exist.

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:46.430
Some of these can be a data
access, data processing tools.

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:51.200
SQL has been around forever,
at least in computer years.

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:54.830
And beyond that, there's tons
of larger scale data processing

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:55.530
tools.

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:58.294
At Stitch Fix we don't
frequently have to use them.

00:12:58.294 --> 00:12:59.960
We do have a Spark
cluster and we use it

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:01.187
for a variety of things.

00:13:01.187 --> 00:13:03.020
But it's pretty rare
for our data scientists

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:05.040
to build models that
are distributed.

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:07.926
But we do have, if you count
all of our data together,

00:13:07.926 --> 00:13:09.800
a large amount of data
that people are moving

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:11.010
kind of all over the place.

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:13.010
There's a lot of different
tools that interlock.

00:13:13.010 --> 00:13:16.430
But I would say there's data
access, client libraries,

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:19.450
and then cluster resource
scheduling kind of tools

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:21.950
that people can actually use
it in our cloud infrastructure.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:22.625
MELANIE WARRICK: I
know, Juliet, you

00:13:22.625 --> 00:13:25.220
were talking about how do
science is seen at Stitch Fix.

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:28.010
Would you also say that
your data from engineering,

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:30.280
is that seen as more
like data engineering?

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:33.440
Is that also classified under
the data science umbrella?

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:34.730
How do you guys partner?

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:37.500
JULIET HOUGLAND: The way that
data science and data platform

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:39.590
interact at Stitch
fix is that we

00:13:39.590 --> 00:13:42.494
expect data scientists to own
their workflows end to end.

00:13:42.494 --> 00:13:44.660
And this is pretty unique
because a lot of companies

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:47.051
have data engineers that
hook up the pipes to make

00:13:47.051 --> 00:13:48.800
sure the data's getting
to the right place

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:50.591
in the right formats
so data scientists can

00:13:50.591 --> 00:13:51.572
do their modeling.

00:13:51.572 --> 00:13:53.030
And we think that
there's something

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:55.250
that you trade in
a system like that.

00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:58.280
What you're trading is the
data will get there reliably

00:13:58.280 --> 00:13:59.720
in whatever format
it gets there.

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:02.096
But then there's this
interaction and handoff

00:14:02.096 --> 00:14:03.970
where a data scientists
will model something,

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:06.510
and they realize that the
pipeline needs to be changed.

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:08.010
And so they go back to
the data engineer and say,

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:09.080
hey, you need to
go fix this thing.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:10.329
You need to change the format.

00:14:10.329 --> 00:14:12.380
And we don't want
head off like that.

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:14.490
A lot of statistics
data science is--

00:14:14.490 --> 00:14:16.250
Stitch Fix algorithms
in general is really

00:14:16.250 --> 00:14:18.560
focused on autonomy
and ownership

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:21.920
and we design the organization
for quick iteration on models.

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:25.010
And that means not having
handoff between data platform

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:26.119
and data science.

00:14:26.119 --> 00:14:28.160
And so the reason that
we're on the data platform

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:29.784
and we're not data
engineers is that we

00:14:29.784 --> 00:14:32.900
build tools to allow data
scientists to be self-service

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:35.345
and to be independent in
owning their workflows.

00:14:35.345 --> 00:14:37.970
And so that's a pretty different
model than a lot of companies,

00:14:37.970 --> 00:14:38.780
and it's one of
the reasons I think

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:40.950
it's pretty interesting to
work there building tools.

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:41.120
MICHELE CASBON: Yeah.

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:43.417
I feel like that approach
is really pioneering.

00:14:43.417 --> 00:14:45.250
I didn't really know a
whole lot about that,

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:47.166
but sort of independently
arrived at something

00:14:47.166 --> 00:14:50.210
very similar at the previous
company that I worked for.

00:14:50.210 --> 00:14:52.970
That sort of enablement
and empowering

00:14:52.970 --> 00:14:54.890
all of your engineers
and data scientists

00:14:54.890 --> 00:14:57.604
to be able to deploy their
own stuff from conception,

00:14:57.604 --> 00:15:00.020
from the very beginning, all
the way through to production

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:02.286
and then to iterate on that,
that saves so much time

00:15:02.286 --> 00:15:04.910
and not having that handoff, not
having that human interaction,

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:07.850
and having ask for permission
and being added to a pipeline

00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:11.570
or queue, like, managing that
human resource side of things,

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:13.640
I felt like that was
very transformative

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:17.200
and just being able to iterate
on models so much more quickly

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:18.880
and not having
them get stale, not

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:21.626
having them be sort of out
of date already by the time

00:15:21.626 --> 00:15:22.750
they make it to production.

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:25.680
That ownership and that
empowerment is really critical.

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:29.060
I feel like you guys
have such a huge custom,

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:30.880
sort of, hand-built
architecture.

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:33.610
You have your own
system that manages

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:35.240
all that, that supports that.

00:15:35.240 --> 00:15:37.320
But I also think
that it's something,

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:39.300
like for me, Kubernetes
really enabled that.

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:41.860
And it's also something that
you don't need an entire team

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:42.970
to do for you.

00:15:42.970 --> 00:15:45.700
Like, I feel like the
tools are there in order

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:46.820
to enable that process.

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:49.660
And you don't have to be like
a sophisticated data science

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:50.994
institution in order to do that.

00:15:50.994 --> 00:15:53.368
MELANIE WARRICK: What do you
think are some of the bigger

00:15:53.368 --> 00:15:55.090
challenges in this space?

00:15:55.090 --> 00:15:57.550
JULIET HOUGLAND: I think in
our particular situation,

00:15:57.550 --> 00:16:01.540
the self-service model, it
is good in a variety of ways,

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:04.682
but there are obvious drawbacks
and sort of trade-offs, right?

00:16:04.682 --> 00:16:06.890
When experienced data
engineers are the ones building

00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:11.500
the pipelines, then you're going
to have more robust pipelines.

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:13.330
And so trying to
figure out systems

00:16:13.330 --> 00:16:16.450
and tooling to make it easier
to build robust pipelines,

00:16:16.450 --> 00:16:19.120
in our case, is definitely
a big challenge.

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:22.076
How do we allow scientists
to be self-service and still

00:16:22.076 --> 00:16:23.950
sort of push them in
the direction of highest

00:16:23.950 --> 00:16:26.800
quality possible and highest
reliability possible?

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:29.980
And that's a problem
that's specific to us.

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:33.220
But when I think about problems
in general that a lot of people

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:37.630
are facing and thinking about,
scheduling, complex workflows,

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:40.270
and versioning
complex workflows,

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:42.580
I think having
reproducibility of models

00:16:42.580 --> 00:16:45.820
is very related to being able to
understand where data came from

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:48.400
and where model artifacts
or dashboards came from

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:51.020
and what definitions
are being used.

00:16:51.020 --> 00:16:52.540
And this is a pretty
common problem

00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:54.880
that ends up being talked
about in the hallway

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:56.477
track of conferences, right?

00:16:56.477 --> 00:16:58.060
People at data
engineering conferences

00:16:58.060 --> 00:17:01.120
will begin to discuss
issues that they have.

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:03.610
And often it'll will
be, oh, this open source

00:17:03.610 --> 00:17:05.140
project is always falling over.

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:07.510
Or what is this data, and
where did it come from?

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:09.940
And how do you go about
providing that visibility

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:10.595
to users?

00:17:10.595 --> 00:17:11.470
MICHELE CASBON: Yeah.

00:17:11.470 --> 00:17:15.359
I feel like the larger story
is the sort of dirty secret

00:17:15.359 --> 00:17:18.369
of data science just
because people really

00:17:18.369 --> 00:17:20.230
want to talk about the
algorithms themselves

00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:22.000
and what cool techniques
they're using.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:24.550
But I feel like the bigger
problem, and the place where

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:27.339
people spend more time,
is just on the system

00:17:27.339 --> 00:17:29.970
as a whole and not that one
machine learning component.

00:17:29.970 --> 00:17:31.750
I feel like that's a
much smaller piece.

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:33.550
And stitching all
those pieces together

00:17:33.550 --> 00:17:36.100
is a non-trivial problem
for reproducibility sake,

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:37.840
for interpretability
sake, for being

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:40.385
able to actually trace back,
here's a result that we got.

00:17:40.385 --> 00:17:42.760
But where did it come from
and how did we arrive on that?

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:45.580
There are a lot of reasons why
you might want to know that.

00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:47.140
The other challenge
I see is just

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:50.674
getting things from that
sort of experimental phase

00:17:50.674 --> 00:17:52.840
into production without
having a whole lot of things

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:56.230
change in between, so being able
to like work locally and then

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:58.210
transform that into
something running on a dev

00:17:58.210 --> 00:18:00.709
cluster and then into production
and being able to make sure

00:18:00.709 --> 00:18:03.280
that it's the same
model that was intended

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:05.150
by the first person
who first built it.

00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:06.697
And that whole
empowerment and being

00:18:06.697 --> 00:18:08.530
able to deploy your own
models, I feel like,

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:10.320
really helps to solve that.

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:11.736
MELANIE WARRICK:
And is that where

00:18:11.736 --> 00:18:14.230
tools like using something
like Kubernetes, Kubeflow

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:16.450
to be able to help
with reproducibility,

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:18.920
as well as moving it
from local to global

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:20.080
would potentially come in?

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:21.080
MICHELE CASBON: Exactly.

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:22.755
Yeah, that's a big
idea behind Kubeflow.

00:18:22.755 --> 00:18:25.630
It supports the whole machine
learning application, so

00:18:25.630 --> 00:18:28.354
as a single unit
instead of trying

00:18:28.354 --> 00:18:29.770
to deploy lots of
different pieces

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:31.180
and lots of different places.

00:18:31.180 --> 00:18:32.554
The idea behind
it is essentially

00:18:32.554 --> 00:18:35.290
to take that entire
application, so not just the ML,

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:38.980
but the logging, the
monitoring, the front end, all

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:41.470
of the auditing, all of
the versioning, everything

00:18:41.470 --> 00:18:45.130
packaging up into one place and
being able to run that locally

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:47.470
for yourself, being
able to access that

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:48.990
through a JupyterHub.

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:53.440
So having other people access it
in a single component cluster,

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:55.510
but then being able to
take that exact same code,

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:57.051
change a couple
environment commands,

00:18:57.051 --> 00:18:59.920
and run it in the cloud,
in a different cloud,

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:04.120
on a completely different set of
physical hardware, or on prem,

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:05.920
or any combination of that.

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:07.739
It's meant to
solve that problem.

00:19:07.739 --> 00:19:09.280
JULIET HOUGLAND: So
I think that gets

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:11.710
at a large part of the
reproducibility problem

00:19:11.710 --> 00:19:13.930
of the environment and the
code that you're running.

00:19:13.930 --> 00:19:15.460
But one of the
biggest challenges

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:20.110
that I've seen in any type
of statistical learned model

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:21.910
is the data part.

00:19:21.910 --> 00:19:25.240
The thing that makes these
sort of systems challenging

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:28.570
for reproducibility is the
tight coupling between the data

00:19:28.570 --> 00:19:30.220
that you're using,
like what data did

00:19:30.220 --> 00:19:32.950
you use to build this model?

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:35.470
Or what data is it
expecting in, and what kind

00:19:35.470 --> 00:19:37.210
of statistical
distribution is it

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:39.920
good at producing
predictions for?

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:41.050
| a tight coupling there.

00:19:41.050 --> 00:19:45.160
And I think that systems that
help with the environment

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:47.200
and help with the
software piece of it

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:49.750
but trying to understand the
connection to that data that

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:53.030
is actually being used
is a big, big challenge.

00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:55.390
And it's not something
that is solved in a way

00:19:55.390 --> 00:19:57.830
that the industry in general
can point to and be like,

00:19:57.830 --> 00:19:59.710
ah, yes, this is the
industry best practice.

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:01.300
This is something that everyone
talks about and doesn't

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:03.670
have a good solution to,
as far as I've heard.

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:05.086
MICHELE CASBON:
Yeah, so I've seen

00:20:05.086 --> 00:20:07.330
a couple of tools come
up pretty recently that I

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:08.334
haven't tried out.

00:20:08.334 --> 00:20:09.750
Kubeflow supports
one of them one.

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:10.840
One I think is Pachyderm.

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:12.490
And then another is Quilt Data.

00:20:12.490 --> 00:20:13.550
And both of them--

00:20:13.550 --> 00:20:14.830
I've looked into them but
just haven't used them

00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:15.950
in a production capacity.

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:18.190
And they're trying to
solve that exact problem.

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:20.148
JULIET HOUGLAND: I've
only looked at Pachyderm.

00:20:20.148 --> 00:20:21.820
And I really like
the idea behind it.

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:26.062
But its version of data
versioning is very heavyweight.

00:20:26.062 --> 00:20:27.520
From what I saw,
what you do is you

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:29.980
bring your data
into a container,

00:20:29.980 --> 00:20:31.340
and then you version it there.

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:33.190
And so basically
Pachyderm works, but only

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:35.317
if everything is inside
this universe of Pachyderm.

00:20:35.317 --> 00:20:36.400
MICHELE CASBON: Mm, I see.

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:38.290
JULIET HOUGLAND: And I see that
as very heavyweight and not

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:39.873
something that people
can easily adopt

00:20:39.873 --> 00:20:41.830
in systems that exist already.

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:42.250
MICHELE CASBON: Interesting.

00:20:42.250 --> 00:20:44.833
JULIET HOUGLAND: And perhaps if
you're building something new,

00:20:44.833 --> 00:20:47.540
like brand new, or you're
willing to make that sacrifice

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:50.230
and move it entirely
into that type of system,

00:20:50.230 --> 00:20:51.350
I see it as heavyweight.

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:53.702
But I haven't dug deeper
into actually using it.

00:20:53.702 --> 00:20:55.660
MICHELE CASBON: Yeah,
I'd be interested to hear

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:57.769
how Stitch Fix solves that
problem in the future.

00:20:57.769 --> 00:20:59.310
MELANIE WARRICK:
Well, and I actually

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:00.850
was going to ask
that in terms of I

00:21:00.850 --> 00:21:03.010
know you don't know exactly
what the best practices are.

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:05.010
This is something people
are trying to go after.

00:21:05.010 --> 00:21:07.030
What are some best
practices or ways

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:08.600
that you guys are
trying to approach

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:10.391
these types of problems
for reproducibility

00:21:10.391 --> 00:21:13.070
that you think would be good to
see others attempt, or at least

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:14.329
is useful for your group?

00:21:14.329 --> 00:21:16.120
JULIET HOUGLAND: So
we're thinking about it

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:20.230
in terms of decomposing
the problem first into data

00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:23.110
versioning and then
artifact versioning.

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:25.132
So what is this data,
and how did it get here?

00:21:25.132 --> 00:21:26.590
What is this process
that generated

00:21:26.590 --> 00:21:29.620
and the sense of being very
deeply linked to data lineage.

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:34.310
And so data lineage, I
think of as this table

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:36.430
and this partition
was the input.

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:38.140
Here is a process
or a job that is

00:21:38.140 --> 00:21:40.599
the thing that used that input.

00:21:40.599 --> 00:21:42.640
And you maybe have a
collection of inputs, right?

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:45.490
And then the output
is a table, columns,

00:21:45.490 --> 00:21:47.284
partitions that this
thing gets written to.

00:21:47.284 --> 00:21:48.700
And so you have
some sort of-- you

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:51.160
need some sort of meta
information that says,

00:21:51.160 --> 00:21:53.650
this is the link
between these tables.

00:21:53.650 --> 00:21:55.820
And this is the version
of the job that ran.

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:57.980
So you version the job,
and you version the data.

00:21:57.980 --> 00:22:00.952
And so that's one way that
we're beginning to look at this.

00:22:00.952 --> 00:22:03.160
That was actually a project
I was working on before I

00:22:03.160 --> 00:22:04.840
started managing this team.

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:08.110
And I decided to put
it on the back burner

00:22:08.110 --> 00:22:10.070
until this team is--

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:12.387
I feel more comfortable
in my role basically.

00:22:12.387 --> 00:22:13.720
MELANIE WARRICK: Understandable.

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:14.950
JULIET HOUGLAND: But
once that is in place,

00:22:14.950 --> 00:22:17.080
I think you need good
data versioning before you

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:17.990
get model versioning.

00:22:17.990 --> 00:22:20.710
And model versioning, I
think, is a holy grail

00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:22.660
that many companies
would like to have.

00:22:22.660 --> 00:22:25.207
You would think many companies
could say that like, oh yes,

00:22:25.207 --> 00:22:26.290
I know what this model is.

00:22:26.290 --> 00:22:27.340
I know what went into it.

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:30.145
And I think you would be
wrong in almost all of them.

00:22:30.145 --> 00:22:33.130
I've definitely heard rumors
of certain types of models

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:35.800
that you would think would be
very resilient and robust not

00:22:35.800 --> 00:22:39.330
being reproducible and having
no way of recreating them

00:22:39.330 --> 00:22:41.080
if a disk drive were
to fail, for example.

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:42.910
I'm sure they're
replicating them.

00:22:42.910 --> 00:22:43.580
MELANIE WARRICK:
We actually have

00:22:43.580 --> 00:22:45.520
the team, the folks who are
working in Project Jupyter

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:46.690
here a few weeks back.

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:48.910
And we talked about
reproducibility

00:22:48.910 --> 00:22:50.829
is one of the goals
for some of the things

00:22:50.829 --> 00:22:52.870
that they're building out
as well, that they know

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:55.360
that that's a clear problem
that's going on in general

00:22:55.360 --> 00:22:57.879
but that the effort to
try to solve for that.

00:22:57.879 --> 00:22:59.670
JULIET HOUGLAND: Well,
I think this ends up

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:02.230
being really related to why
do people do data science?

00:23:02.230 --> 00:23:05.150
I'd say some people do data
science to say that they are,

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:05.650
right?

00:23:05.650 --> 00:23:08.170
There are some companies
that are like, ooh, yeah,

00:23:08.170 --> 00:23:11.380
this is our AI strategy, right?

00:23:11.380 --> 00:23:13.840
And some people do data
science because they want

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:15.550
to deliver value to a business.

00:23:15.550 --> 00:23:18.350
And if what you're trying to do
is deliver value to a business,

00:23:18.350 --> 00:23:19.720
you need to have credibility.

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:21.190
You need to be able to
clearly explain yourself

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:23.648
to business partners and have
them believe that what you've

00:23:23.648 --> 00:23:24.340
made is useful.

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:27.640
And for that you might not
need the holy grail version

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:31.150
of model visibility and
versioning and reproducibility.

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:34.150
But you need some level
of reliability and clarity

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:35.450
about what you've made.

00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:38.140
And I think it's very
easy in data systems

00:23:38.140 --> 00:23:40.590
to end up with a mess
that is unexplainable.

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:43.090
MARK MANDEL: Just so people who
aren't as familiar with data

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:44.798
science get an
understanding, what do you

00:23:44.798 --> 00:23:45.940
mean by model versioning?

00:23:45.940 --> 00:23:46.970
What does that mean?

00:23:46.970 --> 00:23:50.710
JULIET HOUGLAND: Let's say you
have a linear regression model.

00:23:50.710 --> 00:23:52.049
You can build it once.

00:23:52.049 --> 00:23:52.840
You have some data.

00:23:52.840 --> 00:23:54.798
You fit it, and it knows
how to predict things.

00:23:54.798 --> 00:23:57.160
And you're like, great,
I have my model, right?

00:23:57.160 --> 00:23:59.551
In a real production
system, you have this code

00:23:59.551 --> 00:24:00.550
that generates modeling.

00:24:00.550 --> 00:24:02.049
You have the data
that went into it.

00:24:02.049 --> 00:24:03.880
And what you need to
do is apply that code

00:24:03.880 --> 00:24:05.505
that knows how to
train a model and get

00:24:05.505 --> 00:24:07.030
the parameters to new data.

00:24:07.030 --> 00:24:10.180
And so there's maybe two
types of versioning here.

00:24:10.180 --> 00:24:12.100
There's a model that
gets trained again,

00:24:12.100 --> 00:24:14.950
and you get a new collection
of fit parameters.

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:17.345
But the way that you've
trained it hasn't changed.

00:24:17.345 --> 00:24:18.970
And so you probably
want a version that

00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:22.816
and understand what data went
in to create these parameters.

00:24:22.816 --> 00:24:24.940
And then let's say you want
to add another feature.

00:24:24.940 --> 00:24:27.520
This is now different code
and a different structure

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:29.800
of a model and a different
way you've done training.

00:24:29.800 --> 00:24:31.760
And even if you apply
it to the same data,

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:32.856
it's a different model.

00:24:32.856 --> 00:24:34.730
And so there's two types
of versioning there.

00:24:34.730 --> 00:24:36.230
One is that the way
you're producing

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:38.771
the model and the structure of
the model has stayed the same,

00:24:38.771 --> 00:24:39.760
but the data is new.

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:42.340
And the other is the structure
of the model has changed,

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:43.460
perhaps also the data.

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:45.751
And those are two types of
versioning that you probably

00:24:45.751 --> 00:24:46.354
want to track.

00:24:46.354 --> 00:24:48.520
And often it's just sort
of like there are easy ways

00:24:48.520 --> 00:24:51.419
to not do model versioning
and to not keep track of that,

00:24:51.419 --> 00:24:52.960
which will bite you
later, especially

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:55.690
when you end up trying to get
into [INAUDIBLE] make a model.

00:24:55.690 --> 00:24:56.440
Is it any better?

00:24:56.440 --> 00:24:57.465
You need to A/B testing.

00:24:57.465 --> 00:24:59.590
How do you set up a system
that can do A/B testing?

00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:01.734
And how do you know what
went into the two models

00:25:01.734 --> 00:25:02.650
that you're comparing?

00:25:02.650 --> 00:25:04.060
What the difference is.

00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:05.435
MELANIE WARRICK:
We were chatting

00:25:05.435 --> 00:25:07.647
offline a little bit
about the tooling, Julia,

00:25:07.647 --> 00:25:08.980
that I know your group is using.

00:25:08.980 --> 00:25:11.710
Can you explain a little
bit about the type of setup,

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:13.780
especially for the
data-scientist types tools

00:25:13.780 --> 00:25:15.940
that you're setting
up with them for them

00:25:15.940 --> 00:25:17.880
to be able to do the
work that they're doing?

00:25:17.880 --> 00:25:21.040
JULIET HOUGLAND: So we
have cloud infrastructure,

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:25.120
and we allow data
scientists to specify jobs.

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:27.520
Jobs are defined as
Docker containers

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:29.240
running on a cluster.

00:25:29.240 --> 00:25:31.570
So this job will be submitted
on a particular cluster.

00:25:31.570 --> 00:25:34.030
And these jobs are specified
through an open source

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:36.410
tool we have called Flotilla.

00:25:36.410 --> 00:25:38.320
So flotilla is fantastic.

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:39.730
It's super self-service.

00:25:39.730 --> 00:25:41.640
Our data scientists
all really like it.

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:43.974
We actually just did a survey
of the data platform tools

00:25:43.974 --> 00:25:45.848
and got information from
the data scientists.

00:25:45.848 --> 00:25:48.180
And we asked like, what tool
would you miss the most?

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:51.110
40% said Flotilla,
which is great.

00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:55.210
And so Flotilla allows data
scientists to use a UI to say,

00:25:55.210 --> 00:25:56.980
I want to use this Docker image.

00:25:56.980 --> 00:25:58.397
This is the command
I want to run.

00:25:58.397 --> 00:26:00.521
Here's some environment
variables that often people

00:26:00.521 --> 00:26:03.370
will use to define a git repo
that they want to pull from

00:26:03.370 --> 00:26:05.020
and the branch they want to use.

00:26:05.020 --> 00:26:07.210
And then we have a
scheduling system

00:26:07.210 --> 00:26:09.100
that integrates with that.

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:11.980
And that scheduling
system says, combine,

00:26:11.980 --> 00:26:16.690
like make this DAG of jobs and
run it every day at midnight.

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:20.620
Or the first step of those DAG
is wait for data to appear.

00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:24.070
And once this data appears, then
run the rest of this workflow.

00:26:24.070 --> 00:26:27.520
And so that ends up being
what our self-service ETL

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:29.680
definition and scheduling
system looks like.

00:26:29.680 --> 00:26:32.920
And data scientists are pretty
happy with that workflow.

00:26:32.920 --> 00:26:36.340
We have tools that are somewhat
custom for building Docker

00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:37.810
images because it
came from a world

00:26:37.810 --> 00:26:43.000
where we needed to build RPMs,
machine images, and Docker

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:46.549
images, where that
is a little harder

00:26:46.549 --> 00:26:47.840
to use for our data scientists.

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:49.214
So we're thinking
of ways that we

00:26:49.214 --> 00:26:53.140
can make that simpler, use more
open source tools, hopefully

00:26:53.140 --> 00:26:55.116
just give them Docker
files to define.

00:26:55.116 --> 00:26:56.240
MELANIE WARRICK: I like it.

00:26:56.240 --> 00:26:58.269
Michelle, from your
work in the past, what

00:26:58.269 --> 00:27:00.310
have been some of the
pipelines that you've seen,

00:27:00.310 --> 00:27:02.110
or what are some ones
that are successful

00:27:02.110 --> 00:27:03.202
from your perspective?

00:27:03.202 --> 00:27:05.410
MICHELE CASBON: So I was
working for a company called

00:27:05.410 --> 00:27:06.490
Cordoba previously.

00:27:06.490 --> 00:27:08.480
It's a startup based
here in San Francisco.

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:12.880
And it's essentially a B2B SaaS
platform with machine learning

00:27:12.880 --> 00:27:13.670
components.

00:27:13.670 --> 00:27:16.750
And so there was sort
of the normal, sort of,

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:20.560
back-end web component of,
like, what you would normally

00:27:20.560 --> 00:27:23.770
consider like a typical
web architecture,

00:27:23.770 --> 00:27:25.540
typical microservices
architecture.

00:27:25.540 --> 00:27:27.250
And then when we
added the machine

00:27:27.250 --> 00:27:29.710
learning components to that,
they looked very different.

00:27:29.710 --> 00:27:32.697
They didn't have that sort of
standard thousands of companies

00:27:32.697 --> 00:27:34.780
have already built an
architecture that looks just

00:27:34.780 --> 00:27:35.280
like this.

00:27:35.280 --> 00:27:37.840
There are these weird
machine learning components

00:27:37.840 --> 00:27:40.360
that we're kind of trying
to tack on to that.

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:42.280
And the way we
solved that problem

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:45.670
is to take things from this
very homogeneous platform

00:27:45.670 --> 00:27:48.850
and put it all into a managed
Kubernetes environment, where

00:27:48.850 --> 00:27:51.970
we can run everything together
because it's essentially one

00:27:51.970 --> 00:27:52.670
application.

00:27:52.670 --> 00:27:56.470
It's one cohesive
platform for users.

00:27:56.470 --> 00:27:58.450
And by putting that
all in the same place,

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:01.030
we were able to utilize
these very different resource

00:28:01.030 --> 00:28:03.070
requirements but have
everything in sort

00:28:03.070 --> 00:28:06.610
of a self-contained,
single, manageable space

00:28:06.610 --> 00:28:08.660
that you could wrap
your head around

00:28:08.660 --> 00:28:11.980
and then also enable the person
writing the machine learning

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:14.890
code to also deploy all
the way through production

00:28:14.890 --> 00:28:16.960
and iterate very easily.

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:20.770
But even that still required
specific Docker containers

00:28:20.770 --> 00:28:25.000
for each one of those components
for the ingress, for back end,

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:28.870
for search, for the
featurization component,

00:28:28.870 --> 00:28:31.120
for data, for all of that.

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:35.200
And that took
awhile to build out,

00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:38.540
and it ran very smoothly
once it was in place.

00:28:38.540 --> 00:28:42.530
But I took a lot of what I
learned from that experience,

00:28:42.530 --> 00:28:43.640
and it really--

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:46.360
I do want to underscore the fact
that it just really transformed

00:28:46.360 --> 00:28:48.640
the DevOps experience
at that company.

00:28:48.640 --> 00:28:52.122
Like, it took what was a very
onerous sort of just, kind of,

00:28:52.122 --> 00:28:54.580
scattered thing-- things were
in lots of different places--

00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:57.850
and it just put it into this
one, little neatly packaged

00:28:57.850 --> 00:29:01.060
collection, a place you could
really operate on much more

00:29:01.060 --> 00:29:05.420
easily and standardize across
all of the different pieces.

00:29:05.420 --> 00:29:07.330
But it took a lot of
what I learned there,

00:29:07.330 --> 00:29:10.720
and I'm applying that now to
building this Kubeflow project.

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:13.570
And the idea is
the same, where you

00:29:13.570 --> 00:29:16.090
have all of these different
pieces of a machine learning

00:29:16.090 --> 00:29:18.340
application, and you want
them all in one place

00:29:18.340 --> 00:29:20.290
because you want to be
able to version them.

00:29:20.290 --> 00:29:21.998
You want to be able
to move them from one

00:29:21.998 --> 00:29:24.370
environment to the other
and iterate through

00:29:24.370 --> 00:29:26.770
during the development
process and do that in a way

00:29:26.770 --> 00:29:27.700
that scales.

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:30.130
And as new hardware comes out--

00:29:30.130 --> 00:29:34.180
I think you guys talked recently
about new VMs that came out

00:29:34.180 --> 00:29:36.580
with just ridiculous
amounts of RAM and CPU

00:29:36.580 --> 00:29:39.160
and being able to not
have to change your code

00:29:39.160 --> 00:29:40.580
but still be able
to utilize those

00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:43.540
and basically just deploy
it to a new cluster,

00:29:43.540 --> 00:29:46.420
a big piece of that was
just using managed services

00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:48.850
instead of doing
everything from scratch.

00:29:48.850 --> 00:29:51.160
Like originally,
I believe Cordoba

00:29:51.160 --> 00:29:55.140
was sort of rolling their own
open source frameworks on VMs

00:29:55.140 --> 00:29:56.650
and managing that independently.

00:29:56.650 --> 00:30:00.320
And that took a lot
of people hours.

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:02.630
It took a lot of,
sort of, babysitting.

00:30:02.630 --> 00:30:05.380
And by switching to managed
Kubernetes, a lot of that

00:30:05.380 --> 00:30:05.890
went away.

00:30:05.890 --> 00:30:08.890
A lot of the network headaches,
the like hard-coded IP

00:30:08.890 --> 00:30:12.730
addresses, a lot of that just
sort of dissolves into some

00:30:12.730 --> 00:30:14.980
of the features that you get
from a managed Kubernetes

00:30:14.980 --> 00:30:16.040
environment.

00:30:16.040 --> 00:30:17.840
So the aim of Kubeflow
is to really take

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:20.930
all of the benefits, everything
that Kubernetes does really

00:30:20.930 --> 00:30:24.650
well, and sort of apply that to
a machine learning application.

00:30:24.650 --> 00:30:27.860
So that's sort of where I see
the future of machine learning

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:31.920
heading is into this
declarative way of defining

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:32.630
your application.

00:30:32.630 --> 00:30:34.700
And you just sort of
define the resources

00:30:34.700 --> 00:30:37.340
that you need for each
piece, and then you're

00:30:37.340 --> 00:30:40.160
less tied to the physical
hardware that it's running on.

00:30:40.160 --> 00:30:43.277
You can just say, hey, I
want GPUs for this piece.

00:30:43.277 --> 00:30:45.110
You have all the different
types of hardware

00:30:45.110 --> 00:30:46.110
all in the same cluster.

00:30:46.110 --> 00:30:47.360
And you don't really
have to think about it.

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:49.500
And Kubernetes and Kubeflow
manages that for you.

00:30:49.624 --> 00:30:50.540
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:30:50.540 --> 00:30:52.540
Coming back a little bit
to when we were talking

00:30:52.540 --> 00:30:55.340
about the challenge
of reproducibility,

00:30:55.340 --> 00:30:57.920
do you have any perspective
on what can make people

00:30:57.920 --> 00:31:02.390
feel confident in what is
being used now for models?

00:31:02.390 --> 00:31:05.420
Like if it's hard to
reproduce, is it just,

00:31:05.420 --> 00:31:07.820
well, we just have to
believe that it's fine

00:31:07.820 --> 00:31:08.880
and move forward.

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:09.380
Or--

00:31:09.380 --> 00:31:10.970
JULIET HOUGLAND: I find
that most people just

00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:12.230
don't question it, right?

00:31:12.230 --> 00:31:15.290
There's this blind faith and
like, oh, this is mathematical.

00:31:15.290 --> 00:31:16.209
This is analytical.

00:31:16.209 --> 00:31:17.000
It must be correct.

00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:18.110
It must be true.

00:31:18.110 --> 00:31:19.100
And I--

00:31:19.100 --> 00:31:20.360
MELANIE WARRICK: You think
they should question more.

00:31:20.360 --> 00:31:21.530
MARK MANDEL: [LAUGHS].

00:31:21.530 --> 00:31:22.100
JULIET HOUGLAND: Well--

00:31:22.100 --> 00:31:23.880
MELANIE WARRICK: They should
consider questioning more.

00:31:23.880 --> 00:31:26.510
JULIET HOUGLAND: I think
that business partners that

00:31:26.510 --> 00:31:30.410
are coming from less of
an analytical data science

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:33.050
background have
their own questions

00:31:33.050 --> 00:31:35.360
and are often too
easily convinced

00:31:35.360 --> 00:31:36.350
by this is analytical.

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:37.225
This is mathematical.

00:31:37.225 --> 00:31:38.390
It must be true.

00:31:38.390 --> 00:31:40.970
I similarly think that data
science practitioners also

00:31:40.970 --> 00:31:44.020
get kind of caught up in
that mode of thinking,

00:31:44.020 --> 00:31:45.020
that this is analytical.

00:31:45.020 --> 00:31:45.895
This is mathematical.

00:31:45.895 --> 00:31:47.420
It must be true, right?

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:51.230
And really effective
data scientists

00:31:51.230 --> 00:31:53.420
that I've seen tend
to question more

00:31:53.420 --> 00:31:55.790
and tend to sort of
work in a simpler mode

00:31:55.790 --> 00:31:58.826
so that they can circle
back on that question of,

00:31:58.826 --> 00:32:00.200
am I building
something valuable?

00:32:00.200 --> 00:32:01.450
Am I making something correct?

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:03.074
Is this something believable?

00:32:03.074 --> 00:32:05.240
Because once you get into
the technical nitty-gritty

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:07.614
and build a complex model,
it's easy to convince yourself

00:32:07.614 --> 00:32:10.050
that, oh, the ROC is good.

00:32:10.050 --> 00:32:12.110
And so I'm not going to
worry about it, yeah.

00:32:12.110 --> 00:32:14.270
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah, we did
a podcast machine learning bias

00:32:14.270 --> 00:32:15.710
and fairness awhile back, too.

00:32:15.710 --> 00:32:17.668
I think that definitely
plays a role here, too.

00:32:17.668 --> 00:32:20.390
You don't want to just blindly
follow that this model's fine.

00:32:20.390 --> 00:32:22.280
JULIET HOUGLAND: There's not
great automated tools for it,

00:32:22.280 --> 00:32:22.779
right?

00:32:22.779 --> 00:32:25.100
Like, this is why data
scientists, data science

00:32:25.100 --> 00:32:26.840
practitioners with
a lot of experience

00:32:26.840 --> 00:32:28.798
that are thoughtful about
what they're building

00:32:28.798 --> 00:32:29.810
are really valuable.

00:32:29.810 --> 00:32:32.060
They'll take a step back, and
they'll think about have I made

00:32:32.060 --> 00:32:32.750
something good?

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:35.000
Have I made something that
this business will find viable

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:35.660
for a long time?

00:32:35.660 --> 00:32:36.784
Meaning is it reproducible?

00:32:36.784 --> 00:32:37.869
Is it explainable?

00:32:37.869 --> 00:32:39.410
MARK MANDEL: So to
that point, if you

00:32:39.410 --> 00:32:41.060
had sort of a magic
wand, or either of you

00:32:41.060 --> 00:32:42.270
had a magic wand,
where you're like,

00:32:42.270 --> 00:32:44.437
this is the tool that I
would love to have right now

00:32:44.437 --> 00:32:46.978
and I could just make it with
this magic wand, what would you

00:32:46.978 --> 00:32:47.510
make?

00:32:47.510 --> 00:32:50.051
MICHELE CASBON: I mean, I would
love something that just spit

00:32:50.051 --> 00:32:51.447
out the right model every time.

00:32:51.447 --> 00:32:52.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:52.860 --> 00:32:54.800
It does all of the
developmental work for me.

00:32:54.800 --> 00:32:56.762
Like, this is what your
featurization pipeline

00:32:56.762 --> 00:32:57.470
should look like.

00:32:57.470 --> 00:32:59.540
These are all hyper
parameters you should have.

00:32:59.540 --> 00:33:00.990
That would be amazing.

00:33:00.990 --> 00:33:02.970
I mean, I think we're
working towards where

00:33:02.970 --> 00:33:04.820
we're trying to provide that.

00:33:04.820 --> 00:33:07.940
A lot of the managed services,
the point is give us your data,

00:33:07.940 --> 00:33:10.340
and we'll try and fit
the best model for you.

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:12.260
I mean, that seems
pretty magical,

00:33:12.260 --> 00:33:14.360
and like that day is
not quite here yet.

00:33:14.360 --> 00:33:16.752
But I feel like we're
making steps towards that.

00:33:16.752 --> 00:33:18.710
JULIET HOUGLAND: I would
say a sort of listener

00:33:18.710 --> 00:33:21.920
in a data science environment
that watches what steps a data

00:33:21.920 --> 00:33:24.020
scientist takes and sort
of automatically makes

00:33:24.020 --> 00:33:25.186
a lab notebook.

00:33:25.186 --> 00:33:26.810
Every good scientist
has a lab notebook

00:33:26.810 --> 00:33:29.330
where they're making notes about
the environment they're in,

00:33:29.330 --> 00:33:31.760
their experiments, their
setup, what happened that day.

00:33:31.760 --> 00:33:33.350
I think something
that is automated

00:33:33.350 --> 00:33:35.030
that watches and
sort of generates

00:33:35.030 --> 00:33:37.692
a lab notebook for a data
scientists would be my dream.

00:33:37.692 --> 00:33:39.650
MELANIE WARRICK: I actually
was at a conference

00:33:39.650 --> 00:33:43.190
last week on disaster
risk recovery.

00:33:43.190 --> 00:33:45.380
And I noticed one of the
key things that was really

00:33:45.380 --> 00:33:47.630
a challenge for these
organizations that are trying

00:33:47.630 --> 00:33:51.650
to understand and anticipate
and plan and analyze

00:33:51.650 --> 00:33:54.620
for potential natural
disasters is just

00:33:54.620 --> 00:33:56.000
asking the right question.

00:33:56.000 --> 00:33:57.680
Like, what is the
real problem you

00:33:57.680 --> 00:34:01.209
can try to solve with technology
to be able to apply it?

00:34:01.209 --> 00:34:03.000
It would be great if
you just had something

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:05.000
that could spit out
the model, like I think

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:06.590
what is the real question?

00:34:06.590 --> 00:34:08.360
And then what is the
tech and the model

00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:10.550
that I should be using
for that question?

00:34:10.550 --> 00:34:13.670
JULIET HOUGLAND: So previously
I had worked at Cloudera.

00:34:13.670 --> 00:34:15.989
And I did data science there.

00:34:15.989 --> 00:34:18.350
And I would occasionally
go out to big customers

00:34:18.350 --> 00:34:21.000
and to talk with them about
their data science problems.

00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:24.230
So once I went out
to a large customer.

00:34:24.230 --> 00:34:26.870
And they have call centers.

00:34:26.870 --> 00:34:28.670
And so what they
had done was they

00:34:28.670 --> 00:34:32.060
had transcribed calls
to their call centers,

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:34.892
and so they now had
text instead of voice.

00:34:34.892 --> 00:34:37.100
And so they brought me in
because they're like, well,

00:34:37.100 --> 00:34:40.670
we have this small
five-person analytics team.

00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:43.280
And we would like for them
to build an NLP model which

00:34:43.280 --> 00:34:47.960
will do entity identification,
entity resolution,

00:34:47.960 --> 00:34:53.090
and from these call logs, pick
out pieces of information that

00:34:53.090 --> 00:34:54.560
are valuable to us
because they had

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:56.999
resources and customers were
calling for these resources.

00:34:56.999 --> 00:34:59.540
And they were only tracking it
if they had the resources they

00:34:59.540 --> 00:35:00.420
could give them.

00:35:00.420 --> 00:35:02.586
But they wanted to do
understand what the demand was

00:35:02.586 --> 00:35:03.920
that they were missing, right?

00:35:03.920 --> 00:35:05.900
And so I talked
with them for a bit.

00:35:05.900 --> 00:35:08.900
And I realize that
their team does not

00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:11.330
have the skills to build
an NLP model like that,

00:35:11.330 --> 00:35:14.651
nor is usually building a model
that complex really the answer.

00:35:14.651 --> 00:35:16.150
And so I talked to
them for a while,

00:35:16.150 --> 00:35:19.180
and I realized that
what they wanted

00:35:19.180 --> 00:35:22.420
was to get the structured
information that the call

00:35:22.420 --> 00:35:24.160
center representatives
were already

00:35:24.160 --> 00:35:26.560
listening to and
putting into a form that

00:35:26.560 --> 00:35:30.190
was getting turned into a SQL
query that hit their databases.

00:35:30.190 --> 00:35:34.450
And so hearing that, I suggested
that instead of transcribing

00:35:34.450 --> 00:35:37.150
their voice to text
and then building

00:35:37.150 --> 00:35:40.710
a very complex set of
stacked NLP models,

00:35:40.710 --> 00:35:42.460
that perhaps what they
could do is instead

00:35:42.460 --> 00:35:43.840
keep the database queries.

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:47.170
And they had spent already a
lot of money and many months

00:35:47.170 --> 00:35:50.320
on this project, and they were
a little taken aback but also

00:35:50.320 --> 00:35:53.311
pretty excited that they weren't
about to waste a ton more.

00:35:53.311 --> 00:35:53.810
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:53.810 --> 00:35:54.630
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's the key part.

00:35:54.630 --> 00:35:55.260
JULIET HOUGLAND: Yeah.

00:35:55.260 --> 00:35:56.770
MICHELE CASBON: Yeah, I
feel like at the beginning

00:35:56.770 --> 00:35:59.302
of every project that is the
number one most crucial thing

00:35:59.302 --> 00:36:00.760
you can do is make
sure that you're

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:01.930
asking the right question.

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:04.690
And then if you have the
right question in mind,

00:36:04.690 --> 00:36:07.240
can you arrive at the
answer with something

00:36:07.240 --> 00:36:10.420
that's very simple and
mathematically straightforward?

00:36:10.420 --> 00:36:13.980
Like, if the answer to that is
no, then try machine learning.

00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:15.769
But the answer is
not machine learning.

00:36:15.769 --> 00:36:17.560
And you certainly
shouldn't start a company

00:36:17.560 --> 00:36:20.950
based on I'm going to do
these stacked NLP models

00:36:20.950 --> 00:36:23.020
or start a project
with that as the goal.

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:25.720
You really don't want to
let technology or techniques

00:36:25.720 --> 00:36:26.637
drive finding answers.

00:36:26.637 --> 00:36:28.136
MELANIE WARRICK:
One question I want

00:36:28.136 --> 00:36:30.880
to ask both of you, what do you
enjoy most about data science

00:36:30.880 --> 00:36:32.512
and data in general,
working with data?

00:36:32.512 --> 00:36:34.720
JULIET HOUGLAND: I like that
it's always a challenge.

00:36:34.720 --> 00:36:36.010
There's so much to learn.

00:36:36.010 --> 00:36:39.370
Using data effectively
is a mixture

00:36:39.370 --> 00:36:43.010
of understanding the technology
available to work with data,

00:36:43.010 --> 00:36:45.700
understanding the mathematical
techniques available,

00:36:45.700 --> 00:36:47.860
and being able to
communicate with people

00:36:47.860 --> 00:36:50.900
and understand what the
problem is in general,

00:36:50.900 --> 00:36:53.410
and I think that third one
being the most important step.

00:36:53.410 --> 00:36:54.951
Because if you just
do the first two,

00:36:54.951 --> 00:36:57.850
you end up with things
like transcribing voice

00:36:57.850 --> 00:37:01.600
into text and building
stacked NLP models.

00:37:01.600 --> 00:37:05.074
But knowing a little bit about
web applications and databases

00:37:05.074 --> 00:37:06.490
and then understanding
the problem

00:37:06.490 --> 00:37:09.610
is this person is transcribing
it, like that kind of gives you

00:37:09.610 --> 00:37:11.350
a good cut off and short cut.

00:37:11.350 --> 00:37:13.240
And so I would say
that the part that I

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:15.880
like best about
working with data

00:37:15.880 --> 00:37:19.210
is needing to constantly
be improving your skills

00:37:19.210 --> 00:37:20.680
on all three fronts.

00:37:20.680 --> 00:37:22.990
And maybe that's a
bad way to phrase it.

00:37:22.990 --> 00:37:26.170
I tend to focus on improving my
skills on one of those fronts

00:37:26.170 --> 00:37:29.281
and then switching between them
over longer periods of time

00:37:29.281 --> 00:37:31.780
and not constantly trying to
push myself in every direction.

00:37:31.780 --> 00:37:34.810
But always having ways to expand
your skill set technologically

00:37:34.810 --> 00:37:38.447
and mathematically, in terms
of humans, I really enjoy that.

00:37:38.447 --> 00:37:39.780
And the people are great mostly.

00:37:39.780 --> 00:37:40.210
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:40.210 --> 00:37:41.410
MELANIE WARRICK:
Mostly-- got it.

00:37:41.410 --> 00:37:43.868
MICHELE CASBON: Yeah, I feel
like one of my favorite things

00:37:43.868 --> 00:37:47.020
is just that there is an
infinite number of directions

00:37:47.020 --> 00:37:48.060
to go in.

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:50.980
I feel like data is something
that seems so straightforward.

00:37:50.980 --> 00:37:52.900
It's pretty tangible.

00:37:52.900 --> 00:37:55.450
It's a thing that should
be well understood

00:37:55.450 --> 00:37:57.550
because we've been working
with it for decades.

00:37:57.550 --> 00:37:59.230
But every time I
go to solve what

00:37:59.230 --> 00:38:00.850
seems like a
straightforward problem,

00:38:00.850 --> 00:38:05.470
you just have so many
avenues to go down.

00:38:05.470 --> 00:38:08.564
And it's like you can go into--

00:38:08.564 --> 00:38:10.480
you can go down so many
different rabbit holes

00:38:10.480 --> 00:38:12.590
and solve the problem in
so many different ways,

00:38:12.590 --> 00:38:14.950
that it is very challenging
and there's never

00:38:14.950 --> 00:38:18.040
a shortage of new things
to learn, of new ways

00:38:18.040 --> 00:38:19.422
to tackle the
problem, and that's

00:38:19.422 --> 00:38:21.130
also like from a
theoretical perspective,

00:38:21.130 --> 00:38:22.610
from a practical perspective.

00:38:22.610 --> 00:38:25.300
I never feel like there's
a lack of things to learn

00:38:25.300 --> 00:38:26.830
and ways to be challenged.

00:38:26.830 --> 00:38:30.400
And the impact that
you can have with that,

00:38:30.400 --> 00:38:32.320
going in any
different direction,

00:38:32.320 --> 00:38:34.630
just feels like you
can change the world

00:38:34.630 --> 00:38:36.820
in so many different
ways, big or small.

00:38:36.820 --> 00:38:37.946
That's most exciting to me.

00:38:37.946 --> 00:38:39.320
MELANIE WARRICK:
So any resources

00:38:39.320 --> 00:38:41.810
that you'd recommend for those
out there interested in data

00:38:41.810 --> 00:38:43.644
science, data engineering,
machine learning?

00:38:43.644 --> 00:38:46.184
MICHELE CASBON: I really love
the machine learning flashcards

00:38:46.184 --> 00:38:47.920
that Chris Albon puts out.

00:38:47.920 --> 00:38:49.030
Those are my favorite.

00:38:49.030 --> 00:38:51.610
They're just these
little hand-drawn--

00:38:51.610 --> 00:38:54.460
like he'll have a formula that
he'll just draw out, literally

00:38:54.460 --> 00:38:55.947
like you would do in college.

00:38:55.947 --> 00:38:58.280
And I think he started out
just making them for himself.

00:38:58.280 --> 00:39:00.610
But there are like 300
now, and he publishes them.

00:39:00.610 --> 00:39:02.650
You can purchase them
for like $10, $15.

00:39:02.650 --> 00:39:04.402
But they're really, really good.

00:39:04.402 --> 00:39:06.610
And just going through a
few of those a day just kind

00:39:06.610 --> 00:39:08.777
of refreshes, like keeps
the neurons firing.

00:39:08.777 --> 00:39:09.610
I really like those.

00:39:09.610 --> 00:39:10.940
It's not a place to start.

00:39:10.940 --> 00:39:12.130
But it is a good way to--

00:39:12.130 --> 00:39:13.390
MELANIE WARRICK:
It's a good resource.

00:39:13.390 --> 00:39:14.590
JULIET HOUGLAND: There's
a lot of different things

00:39:14.590 --> 00:39:16.240
that you can develop
and you can learn,

00:39:16.240 --> 00:39:18.340
and so one of the
best resources I've

00:39:18.340 --> 00:39:20.627
seen that has a sort of
hierarchy and structure

00:39:20.627 --> 00:39:22.960
of the different types of
things you could be developing

00:39:22.960 --> 00:39:25.780
