00:41:46.250 --> 00:41:47.000
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:41:47.000 --> 00:41:49.000
MELANIE WARRICK: If I've
written a gRPC service

00:41:49.000 --> 00:41:52.250
but I'm using a language or
platform that isn't supported,

00:41:52.250 --> 00:41:54.560
is there any way I can
access it as a rest?

00:41:54.560 --> 00:41:56.470
MARK MANDEL: Yeah so
I really like gRPC.

00:41:56.470 --> 00:41:58.230
So if you're not
familiar with gRPC,

00:41:58.230 --> 00:42:00.560
it stands for gRPC
Remote Procedure Call.

00:42:00.560 --> 00:42:01.312
So it's recursive.

00:42:01.312 --> 00:42:02.270
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:42:02.270 --> 00:42:02.860
MARK MANDEL: Always, yeah.

00:42:02.860 --> 00:42:03.690
MELANIE WARRICK: Fantastic.

00:42:03.690 --> 00:42:05.640
MARK MANDEL: It's a great way of
building remote procedure call

00:42:05.640 --> 00:42:06.240
systems.

00:42:06.240 --> 00:42:07.740
It does a lot of
really cool stuff.

00:42:07.740 --> 00:42:09.110
One of the things
that's great about gRPC

00:42:09.110 --> 00:42:11.330
is it sends things like
protobuf over the wire.

00:42:11.330 --> 00:42:12.130
It uses HTTP2.

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:13.796
So it has some
requirements, and there's

00:42:13.796 --> 00:42:15.600
a set of languages
that are supported.

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:17.720
So if you're using a
language or a platform that

00:42:17.720 --> 00:42:20.240
isn't supported, like say maybe
JavaScript on the browser,

00:42:20.240 --> 00:42:22.490
or maybe there's a particular
language it hasn't been built

00:42:22.490 --> 00:42:24.470
for yet, something like
that, you can actually

00:42:24.470 --> 00:42:25.280
expose it as rest.

00:42:25.280 --> 00:42:26.170
And there's a couple
of tools that'll

00:42:26.170 --> 00:42:28.460
let you do that, each it's,
kind of, pros and cons.

00:42:28.460 --> 00:42:29.870
The one that came out first--

00:42:29.870 --> 00:42:32.370
yeah, I'm going to say it came
out first-- is an open source

00:42:32.370 --> 00:42:35.450
product that was in the gRPC
ecosystem repository called

00:42:35.450 --> 00:42:36.980
gRPC Gateway.

00:42:36.980 --> 00:42:40.130
It's literally a
tool and/or a library

00:42:40.130 --> 00:42:41.940
that you can either
do it one of two ways.

00:42:41.940 --> 00:42:44.360
If you've got a gRPC
service written as Go,

00:42:44.360 --> 00:42:46.520
you can basically
use it as a library

00:42:46.520 --> 00:42:48.519
to expose your
endpoints as HTTP.

00:42:48.519 --> 00:42:50.060
You just put some
extra configuration

00:42:50.060 --> 00:42:51.185
in your protofile.

00:42:51.185 --> 00:42:52.310
And you can do it that way.

00:42:52.310 --> 00:42:54.890
It'll also generate Swagger,
do some other nice stuff too.

00:42:54.890 --> 00:42:56.330
But it can also
be used as a proxy

00:42:56.330 --> 00:42:57.420
if you want to do
it that way too.

00:42:57.420 --> 00:42:59.600
So if you have a gRPC service
in a different language

00:42:59.600 --> 00:43:01.266
or you just want to
put a proxy in front

00:43:01.266 --> 00:43:03.922
without having to work in the
same process, you can do that.

00:43:03.922 --> 00:43:05.130
And that works really nicely.

00:43:05.130 --> 00:43:06.710
The other thing that
is also possible

00:43:06.710 --> 00:43:09.000
is you can use another
proxy called Envoy.

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:11.150
Envoy's got a lot of
popularity recently,

00:43:11.150 --> 00:43:13.234
came out of collaborations
between Lyft and Google

00:43:13.234 --> 00:43:15.358
and probably a whole bunch
of other people as well.

00:43:15.358 --> 00:43:16.770
It does a whole bunch of stuff.

00:43:16.770 --> 00:43:18.890
But one of the things
that could also do is also

00:43:18.890 --> 00:43:21.890
do gRPC conversions
to HTTP and JSON,

00:43:21.890 --> 00:43:24.590
so essentially rest very
much, in exactly the same vein

00:43:24.590 --> 00:43:27.520
as gRPC Gateway does actually
as well, which is kind of neat.

00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:29.100
It uses the same configuration.

00:43:29.100 --> 00:43:31.220
And finally, should also
make mention of the fact

00:43:31.220 --> 00:43:32.986
that if you're running
on Google Cloud,

00:43:32.986 --> 00:43:35.360
we have a product called Cloud
Endpoints will actually do

00:43:35.360 --> 00:43:36.960
exactly the same thing as well.

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:40.410
So you can also use that to
convert from gRPC to HTTP

00:43:40.410 --> 00:43:40.910
endpoints.

00:43:40.910 --> 00:43:42.590
And the neat thing
is all three of them

00:43:42.590 --> 00:43:44.048
use exactly the
same configuration.

00:43:44.048 --> 00:43:45.230
So you can actually try one.

00:43:45.230 --> 00:43:47.313
I would say pretty much
switch between the others,

00:43:47.313 --> 00:43:48.690
really, relatively easily.

00:43:48.690 --> 00:43:51.140
We'll add some more
documentation to the show noes

00:43:51.140 --> 00:43:51.890
as well.

